lem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Documentation lifecycle is related but not logging itself. Maybe applicability 3.\n\neffectiveness: degree will solve problem. Reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial. Logging mandate requires technical capability, this is admin process. Helps compliance but doesn't ensure logs technically work. So 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but partial). Or 4? If you could do it would solve main problem? No, main problem is logging mandate technical. This addresses documentation not logs. So 3.\n\noriginality: degree rare ingenious. This is common governance procedure. Common mundane boring ->1. Maybe interesting? Probably 1 = common mundane.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. It introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice? Actually it's slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Or paradigm preserving 1. It adds fields and custodian, so introduces new elements but still standard compliance. Probably 2.\n\nLet's output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably list.\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nEnsure order: implementability, acceptability, applicability, effectiveness, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nProvide for P1977.\n\nLet's craft one sentence evidence each.\n\nImplementability_evidence: Adding two fields to existing intake form and assigning a documentation custodian with quarterly reviews uses existing research-management office processes. Score 3? Might be 4 easy. \"easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\". Adding fields is non-radical. Could be 4. Hmm.\n\nThe description: some changes, or reasonably feasible measures =3. Easy to implement at low cost =4. I think it's easy low cost. Might be 4.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Internal governance and documentation custodianship is a common administrative practice that violates no legal or social norms. Score 4.\n\nApplicability evidence: The protocol addresses risk classification and keeping technical documentation current, which is directly tied to the Act's record-keeping and traceability requirements for high-risk systems. Score 4? Wait earlier thought 3. Let's see definition: applicability 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Documentation lifecycle is part of record-keeping mandate per Recital 71 and Article 12. Logging mandate includes logs but also documentation. Might be directly related. Could be 4.\n\nLet's choose 4 for applicability.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: It ensures documentation stays current through retraining and modification, contributing to compliance and traceability but does not itself build the automatic logging capability required by Article 12(1). Score 3.\n\nOriginality evidence: Flagging high-risk projects at kickoff and assigning a documentation custodian is a standard compliance management approach commonly used in regulated R&D. Score 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal introduces administrative tools and processes but keeps compliance as a separate documentation task rather than redefining how R&D and compliance interrelate. Score 2.\n\nProvide output.\n\n",
