. Could be 4? \"not expressed before (rare, unusual) AND ingenious, imaginative, or surprising\". Studying epistemic problem maybe ingenious. But likely 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = ...\n\nStated problem: lab must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal is research programme to develop framework for selecting logged events. Does that solve logging mandate? It addresses knowledge gap about what to log. It's directly related? Might be implied problem: need to know what to log. Applicability 4? It solves identified problem that is directly related: need to decide how to meet logging mandate. Developing framework for selecting events is directly related. So applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable...\nProposal will not immediately implement logging; it's research. Might contribute but not solve mandate. It may solve knowledge gap but still need implementation. So effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy low cost, non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change, expensive/difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nThree-person team develops framework, publish. That's some changes, reasonably feasible? Requires resources but not huge. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon/unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nResearch programme is acceptable. Might be unusual but acceptable. Probably 4? Common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Doing research is normal. Could be 4. Or 3 somewhat uncommon. I'd say 4.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each then score.\n\nOutput format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nP2810\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure order listed.\n\nProvide concise one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.",
