        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nNeed rate P2370.\n\nProposal: Unified Validation Documentation as Publishable Science. Lab-wide methodology reform elevates technical documentation required by Recital 71 into standardized template doubles as methods section of research publications and internal peer reviews. Research-methods working group drafts single scientific-record schema per project, reviewed at three gates..., so compliance documentation and reproducible-science documentation are same artifact maintained through lifetime. Removes duplication cost...\n\nWe need six subscales.\n\nacceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable\n1 = radically violates laws or sensibilities\n\nThis is about merging compliance docs with publications. Is it acceptable? Likely acceptable, common strategies? Uncommon but doesn't offend. Probably 3? Could be 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Is unifying documentation common? Might be somewhat uncommon. I'd say 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. Evidence: merging regulatory docs with publications does not violate law or norms.\n\nimplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 easy low cost non-radical changes\n3 some changes reasonably feasible\n2 significant change expensive difficult but not impossible\n1 totally infeasible\n\nLab-wide methodology reform, working group drafts schema per project, three gates. That's significant change, requires process redesign. Probably 2? Or 3? \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" vs \"significant change, or expensive or difficult\". Might be significant change. I'd rate 3? Let's think: Need to change templates, workflows, training. Could be reasonably feasible. I think implementability = 3 (some changes reasonably feasible). Maybe 2? I'd go with 3.\n\noriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nUnified documentation as publishable science. Is this original? Might be unusual interesting. Probably 3. Not necessarily never expressed before. I'd say 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\n4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving standard compliance\n\nThis proposal merges compliance documentation with scientific record, changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. That's paradigm stretching? It changes relationship: compliance becomes part of science. Might be 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). Could be 4? Does it introduce new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It addresses duplication cost, raises intellectual rigour. Might be stretching not breaking. I'd rate 3.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 solves identified problem directly related\n3 solves implied problem related\n2 may have some benefit within special situation\n1 intervention not stated\n\nProblem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal addresses technical documentation required by Recital 71, which is part of record-keeping. It does not directly address automatic logging of events per Article 12(1-3). It addresses technical documentation. Might be related but not logging mandate? Recital 71 mentions logs. Article 12 is logging. Proposal focuses on documentation schema for algorithms, data provenance, training validation processes – which is Recital 71 documentation, not automatic logging of events. So applicability maybe 3? Solves implied problem? The stated problem includes meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. It adapts R&D to compliance via unified docs. Might be directly related? Logging mandate is about logs, not documentation. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal mentions technical documentation required by Recital 71—algorithms, data provenance... This is part of record-keeping but Article 12 is logging. However Recital 71 is purpose statement. The problem says must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. The proposal adapts R&D to meet documentation requirements, not logging per se. Could be applicability = 3 solves implied problem related to compliance burden.\n\nMaybe applicability = 2? May have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates. I think it's directly related to record-keeping requirement in Recital 71 which is part of mandate. The logging mandate also includes technical documentation? Article 12 is logging, but Recital 71 mentions logs too. The proposal focuses on documentation not logs.\n\nGiven instrument, I'd rate applicability = 3: solves implied problem that is related – reduces duplication cost for compliance documentation.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 unreasonable unlikely\n1 solves unrelated\n\nWill unified validation documentation solve logging mandate? Not really. It helps with documentation but not automatic recording of events, start/end times, reference database etc. So partial contribution at best. Maybe 3 = contributes to solution of problem (helps with compliance documentation). Or 2 = unlikely to solve problem because logging requirement is different.\n\nThe stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. It does adapt R&D, so it contributes. I'd say effectiveness = 3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial).\n\nNow produce output with evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale in order give ... Provide maybe bullet list.\n\nWe'll output:\n\nid: P2370\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Merging regulatory technical documentation with publication methods does not violate legal norms or offend research sensibilities and aligns with transparency expectations.\nacceptability: 3\n\nimplementability_evidence: Creating a lab-wide schema and three-gate review requires process redesign and cross-team coordination but uses existing publication workflows, making it reasonably feasible.\nimplementability: 3\n\noriginality_evidence: Treating compliance documentation as a publishable scientific record template is unusual and shows imagination beyond standard separate paperwork approaches.\noriginality: 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: By making compliance documentation the same artifact as scientific methods, the proposal changes the relationship between R&D practice and compliance obligations rather than treating logging as a separate administrative task.\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n\napplicability_evidence: The idea directly addresses the Recital 71 documentation burden that accompanies the Article 12 record-keeping mandate and thus applies to how the lab adapts R&D to compliance.\napplicability: 3? Might be 4? Let's keep 3.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Unifying documentation reduces duplication and improves traceability for technical documentation, but it does not provide automatic event logging required by Article 12(1)-(3), so it only partially solves the mandate.\neffectiveness: 3\n\nCould applicability be 4? \"solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\" The stated problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Adapting R&D to compliance is part. So maybe 4.\n\nHmm decide. The proposal text explicitly mentions technical documentation required by Recital 71—algorithms... This is part of record-keeping. Might be directly related. I'd go applicability 4.\n\nLet's choose applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness maybe 3 still.\n\nFinal output.\n\n",
