cability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related. Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Yes. 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common.\n\nIs mandatory logging gate in sprint one original? It's a process change. Might be somewhat uncommon but not ingenious. Probably 2 = interesting? Or 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Hmm. Experienced evaluator would expect labs to give responses like add logging library etc. A sprint gate is maybe unusual. I'd say 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal introduces logging-design deliverable as formal work item, auto-updates via CI hooks. That's introducing new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching. So 2.\n\nCheck ordering: implementability_evidence then implementability, acceptability_evidence then acceptability, effectiveness_evidence then effectiveness, applicability_evidence then applicability, originality_evidence then originality, paradigm_relatedness_evidence then paradigm_relatedness.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly.\n\nProvide JSON? Probably just output. Provide each.\n\nMake one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft: