rement to enable traceability and post-market monitoring under Article 72.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nBuilding internal dashboard is acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Internal observability for compliance logging is a common, norm-conforming strategy.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nIt requires data-engineering team develop centralised log-ingestion pipeline with configurable alert thresholds... That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? Might be significant. Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: Developing a centralized pipeline and dashboard requires dedicated engineering effort and integration across deployed high-risk systems, which is feasible but not trivial.\n\nLet's output in required format.\n\nNeed for each subscale: <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nOrder: paradigm_relatedness, originality, effectiveness, applicability, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide id echo.\n\nReturn rating.\n\nProbably JSON? Probably just lines.\n\nWill output:\n\nid: P2579\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise one sentence each.\n\nParadigm relatedness evidence: It introduces a dashboard and alerting pipeline but still treats compliance as a logging practice rather than redefining R&D or its relationship to compliance.\n\nOriginality evidence: An internal observability dashboard for production logs and risk alerts is a common, mundane industry approach.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The dashboard supports post-market monitoring and traceability but does not itself guarantee the mandated automatic recording of events and specified log content.\n\nApplicability evidence: Ingesting logs to surface