n use it? Could be self-serving.\n\nMaybe effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Because building open source toolkit is extra work beyond just meeting own logs. Might be partial solution.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Building toolkit to meet Article 12 logging requirements is directly related. So applicability 4.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Easy to implement at low cost or non-radical changes? Building modular software framework in Rust/Python with plugin hooks for PyTorch/JAX, document against each sub-clause, publish under open licence with white paper. That's significant engineering effort. Not easy low cost. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 2 = significant change, expensive or difficult but not totally impossible. Building toolkit from scratch is significant. For an R&D lab with scarce resources, committing to build open source framework is major. I'd say implementability 2? \"significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\". Might be 3.\n\nLet's choose 2? Hmm. The proposal says drop-in layer... Requires building. That's significant change. Probably implementability = 2.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Open source toolkit is common, acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. So acceptability 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings