s systems. Score 4? Might be 3. Let's decide effectiveness = 3 (contribute). Because building toolkit is not same as meeting own logging mandate; it creates infrastructure that can be used later.\n\nIf you could do it, would it solve main problem? If you build toolkit, you still need to deploy it for your own high-risk systems; the proposal focuses on community benefit. So partial.\n\nI'll set effectiveness =3.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal explicitly targets Article 12(2) and (3) logging obligations that define the lab's compliance decision. Score 4.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Developing a Rust/Python modular framework with PyTorch/JAX plugins and formal documentation against each sub-clause requires substantial engineering resources beyond routine logging. Score 2.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Publishing an open-licensed compliance toolkit is socially encouraged and creates no legal or political conflict with the AI Act. Score 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The idea reframes compliance from internal engineering burden to a shared scientific contribution, altering how R&D relates to regulatory obligations. Score 3.\n\noriginality_evidence: While open-source tools are common, a community drop-in traceability framework specifically mapped to Article 12 sub-clauses is unusual and shows imagination for this mandate. Score 3.\n\nReturn format.\n\n",
