cle 12(2)(b) post-market monitoring. So applicability =4? It solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem: do dashboard to get monitoring. Yes.\n\nEvidence: The dashboard directly uses the event logs mandated by Article 12 to enable traceability and post-market monitoring as required.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 easy low cost non-radical changes\n3 some changes reasonably feasible\n2 significant change expensive difficult but not impossible\n1 totally infeasible\n\nCreating dedicated monitoring cell, dashboards, pairing analysts with researchers, rule-based + model-assisted anomaly detection... That's significant change, needs new team, integration. Maybe 2 or 3. \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" =3. Probably requires building pipelines, staffing. Could be significant. I'd say 3? Let's say 2? Significant change, expensive/difficult but not impossible. Dedicated cell suggests resource commitment. Might be 2.\n\nLet's pick implementability =2: significant change, or expensive or difficult but not totally impossible.\n\nEvidence: Establishing a dedicated monitoring cell with continuous dashboards and model-assisted anomaly detection requires new staffing, engineering integration, and workflow changes beyond routine logging.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 common strategies violate no norms\n3 somewhat uncommon unusual do not offend sensibilities\n2 offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 radically violates...\n\nThis is standard compliance, acceptable. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Proactive post-market surveillance via log monitoring aligns with regulatory expectations and institutional risk management norms.\n\nNow output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nOrder listed: paradigm_relatedness, originality, effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence then score.\n\