ntifies which projects can comply and where resources should go, but it does not itself create the required logging capabilities. Score 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nCommon portfolio audit for compliance? Might be unusual interesting; shows some imagination? Probably common. Audit and reprioritisation is standard management practice. Common, mundane, boring =1? Maybe interesting =2? Let's think reference class: experienced evaluator expect AI R&D labs to give responses. Audit against regulation is fairly standard. Originality 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 = interesting. I think it's common compliance gap analysis. I'd rate 1.\n\nEvidence: Gap audits and resource reprioritisation are standard compliance management practices routinely used for new regulations. Score 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\nParadigm breaking = introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Paradigm stretching = changes that relationship... slightly paradigm stretching = introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Paradigm preserving = standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged.\n\nAudit is standard compliance practice, treats compliance as logging practice, no change to R&D paradigm. So 1 = paradigm preserving.\n\nEvidence: The proposal keeps R&D and compliance separate by applying standard logging practice and portfolio management without altering how science is conducted. Score 1.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms =4.\n\nEvidence: Structured audits and board-level resource reallocation are normal, legally safe business practices. Score 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low co