dic log audits and aggregate client dashboards are familiar industry practices rather than imaginative.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: paradigm preserving? Introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces audit function and portal but still treats compliance as logging practice. Description: 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2. Evidence: adds audit sampling and client-facing metrics but leaves R&D workflow unchanged.\n\nEffectiveness: reasonable and will contribute to solution? Will verify logging capabilities produce records required, protects reputational damage. It helps ensure compliance but does not itself create logs. The problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This proposal verifies logs exist, doesn't ensure they are produced? Might be partial. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but only partial). Evidence: audit verifies existing logging meets Article 12 requirements but does not create the mandated logging capabilities.\n\nApplicability: solves an identified problem directly related? It addresses traceability/verification. Applicable. 4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). Maybe 3? The stated problem is decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This is about verification, not meeting mandate. Might be implied problem of ensuring compliance. I'd say 4? Hmm.\n\nThe proposal's goal: prove systems genuinely record what we claim protects from reputational damage if regulator inspects. That's directly related to meeting mandate. Applicability 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Evidence: spot-checks and portal directly target Article 12(2) traceability and post-market monitoring requirements.\n\nLet's assign scores:\n\nacceptability_evidence: Internal audits and read-only compliance dashboards are standard, legally permissible practices that do not offend sensibilities.\nacceptability: 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building a semi-annual sampling audit and quarterly portal requires staffing and engineering but uses existing logs and deployments, making it reasonably feasible.\nimplementability: 3\n\noriginality_evidence: Periodic log-integrity spot-checks and aggregate client transparency portals are familiar compliance tools rather than ingenious or surprising.\noriginality: 2\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds audit sampling and reporting processes but keeps compliance as a logging practice without changing how the lab conducts R&D.\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Verifying that deployed systems actually produce Article 12 records helps ensure traceability but does not itself create the required logging capabilities.\neffectiveness: 3\n\napplicability_evidence: The audit and portal directly address the mandate to ensure logging capabilities meet Article 12(2)(a)-(c) for high-risk AI systems.\napplicability: 4\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nOutput format maybe JSON lines. Provide each.\n\nLet's output structured.\n\n",
