hange, or expensive or difficult\nBuilding SaaS module on top of logging middleware, configurable alerting. Requires data-engineering group build. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 3. Not easy low cost? Probably moderate. Let's pick 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nStated problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal builds dashboard using mandatory logs, meets logging? It ingests mandatory logs, surfaces drift... Does it meet logging mandate? It uses logs but doesn't ensure logging itself? It builds on top of logging middleware. Might help with compliance? Applicability is about applying to stated problem: meeting logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Dashboard helps monitor operation and post-market monitoring per Article 12 2(b)(c). So directly related. Maybe 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve the problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\nDashboard doesn't ensure logs are recorded per Article 12 1-3. It assumes logs exist. It may help traceability but not guarantee logging capability. Might be partial solution. So effectiveness 3? Could be 4 if considered solving mandate by making logs useful? The mandate is technically allow automatic recording. Dashboard is extra. Might contribute but not solve core logging mandate. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial).\n\nLet's produce output with evidence sentences then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale in order listed: FIRST write one sentence of evidence... THEN give its score as integer level.\n\nProbably output like:\nid: P2846\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 3\n...\n\nMake sure subscale names exactly: originality, paradigm_relatedness, acceptability, implementability, applicability, effectiveness\n\nProvide evidence one sentence each.\n\nLet's craft evidence sentences.\n\noriginality_evidence: The dashboard productises mandatory logs into a SaaS analytics offering, which is unusual for an R&D lab but resembles existing compliance-as-product plays.\nScore 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It creates a new commercial offering born from compliance and feeds operational insight back into R&D prioritisation, changing how compliance relates to research.\nScore 3? Maybe 4? Hmm. If we chose 3, evidence matches.\n\nAlternatively choose 4 for paradigm breaking. Let's think: The description says paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, OR addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does both. Paradigm stretching example includes new partners or offerings born from compliance. That's exactly this. So paradigm stretching is 3. Breaking might be more radical. I'll keep 3.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Productising logs for downstream deployers with configurable Article 79 and Article 72 alerts does not violate legal norms and aligns with post-market monitoring expectations.\nScore 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building a SaaS module on existing logging middleware requires data-engineering effort and integration work but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab.\nScore 3\n\napplicability_evidence: The panel ingests Article 12 mandatory logs to support traceability, risk-trigger identification, and post-market monitoring as required by the regulation.\nScore 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: While it does not itself create the Article 12 logging capability, it makes mandated logs usable for compliance monitoring and R&D feedback, contributing partially to meeting the mandate.\nScore 3\n\nReturn rating.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence. No extra sentences.\n\nLet's output succinc