ce. Also research track may be overkill? If you could do it, would it solve main problem? It would provide lifetime-persistent logs, which is part of mandate. Might not address content requirements 2(a)-(c). Could be partial.\n\nProbably effectiveness 3: reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Because log integrity is needed but mandate also requires specific logging capabilities. Might still be partial.\n\nEvidence: Developing tamper-evident chained storage improves lifetime traceability but does not by itself ensure recording of the specific event types required by Article 12(2)-(3).\n\nScore 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal creates dedicated R&D workstream to develop log integrity as research track, publish internal standard. That changes relationship? Might be stretching: logging woven into science, new offering born from compliance. It's not breaking paradigm of what lab researches? It introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice? It is a research track on compliance technology, so maybe slightly paradigm stretching? Could argue paradigm stretching: changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations by making compliance a core R&D theme.\n\nDefinition: 3 = changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). This fits: they are making logging a research track, potentially market leader in traceability. So 3.\n\nEvidence: The proposal embeds compliance-driven logging into the lab’s core R&D agenda and frames it as a market-leading capability rather than a mere administrative overhead.\n\nScore 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nTamper-evident cryptographically chained logs is fairly common for audit. Might be interesting but not rare? For AI labs, dedicated research track on log integrity may be unusual. Probably originality 2 or 3.\n\nReference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Expected responses: buy logging tool, add logging middleware. Dedicated R&D workstream with two senior researchers to invent tamper-evident storage is unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. So maybe 3.\n\nEvidence: A lab-initiated research track focused on decade-long forensic log integrity is less common than off-the-shelf logging procurement in typical compliance responses.\n\nScore 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms...\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nDeveloping tamper-evident logs is socially/legal politically acceptable. No violation. Might be uncommon but acceptable. Could be 4? Common strategies that violate no norms. Developing cryptographically chained storage is not common strategy? Acceptability is about social/legal/political acceptability, not commonness. It's acceptable. Level 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Is it common? Might be somewhat uncommon. But acceptability rating: does it violate norms? No. Could be 4.\n\nMaybe 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. The strategy of R&D for compliance is acceptable. I'd give 4.\n\nEvidence: Tamper-evident logging research aligns with regulatory e