liance and logging practice; the lab's way of working is unchanged. The proposal doesn't change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations fundamentally, just prioritizes.\n\nProbably 1.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy? One-time audit with small team six-week sprint. Some changes reasonably feasible. That's 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Assigning 2 engineers +1 legal for six weeks is moderate cost but not huge. Probably 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Yes.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nWill classify projects, allocate engineering hours where legally required instead of uniform overhead. Does that solve logging mandate? It helps target resources but does not actually implement logging. Problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Classification is a prerequisite but not solution. It's reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). So 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial).\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves an identified problem directly related? The stated problem is decide how to meet logging mandate. Classification is directly related: knowing which projects trigger obligations lets allocate. That's 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Possibly 4.\n\nWe need one sentence evidence each.\n\nProvide output with id echoing.\n\nFormat: maybe list.\n\nNeed first write one sentence evidence for that subscale then give its score.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nP2830\noriginality_evidence: ... \noriginality:1\n\netc.\n\nMake sure order: origi