entified problem: how to meet logging mandate. Does audit portal meet logging mandate? No, it verifies logs exist. It doesn't implement logs. So maybe it's implied problem: avoid reputational damage / ensure compliance is real. Hmm.\n\nProbably applicability = 3 (solves implied problem that is related). Because the core mandate is technical logging capability, not auditing.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? No. It will contribute partially. Audit helps ensure logs are produced, but doesn't create logs. So 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could be 2? Unlikely to solve? Might contribute. I'd say 3.\n\nImplementability (1-4): easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? Build internal audit function sampling 20% deployments semi-annual, client portal quarterly metrics. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. So 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Might be moderate. Could be 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Internal audit and transparency portal is common, does not offend. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Possibly 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchang