e main problem? Main problem is meet logging mandate. Governance alone doesn't create logs. So not full solve. So 3.\n\nEvidence sentence: The program governs retention and access of logs but does not create the mandated automatic event recording capabilities required by Article 12(1)-(3).\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? Do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Governance for log retention is related to record-keeping recital 71 about keeping records up to date and availability. Might be directly related. Probably 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem.\n\nEvidence: Retention schedules and access controls directly address Recital 71's requirement to keep technical documentation up to date and ensure traceability throughout system lifetime.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Log retention governance with encryption, RBAC, audit-trail is common. That's 4.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Stand up cross-functional program, embed rules into middleware as enforceable config parameters. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Requires working group and embedding. Probably 3. Could be 4? Easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Might be more than non-radical. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: Establishing a working group and configuring middleware requires coordinated policy and engineering effort but uses standard IT security practices.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm. Options: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science