        "content": "{\"ratings\":[\n{\"id\":\"P2774\",\n\"originality_evidence\":\"Quarterly injection of anomalies to test log fidelity is unusual for AI labs and shows imagination beyond routine compliance checks.\",\n\"originality\":3,\n\"paradigm_relatedness_evidence\":\"The test adds a new QA process but still treats compliance as a logging practice rather than changing how R&D and compliance relate.\",\n\"paradigm_relatedness\":2,\n\"implementability_evidence\":\"Running scripted scenarios quarterly requires QA time and checklists but uses existing systems, making it reasonably feasible.\",\n\"implementability\":3,\n\"acceptability_evidence\":\"Internal audits for regulatory readiness are common and violate no legal, social or business norms.\",\n\"acceptability\":4,\n\"effectiveness_evidence\":\"Verifying log completeness helps ensure Article 12 obligations are met but does not itself create the required automatic logging capability.\",\n\"effectiveness\":3,\n\"applicability_evidence\":\"The stress test directly targets the traceability and logging requirements of Article 12(2) for high-risk systems.\",\n\"applicability\":4\n}\n]}",
        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nWe need rate proposal P2774.\n\nProposal: Quarterly Log-Fidelity Stress Tests. Recurring internal audit inject anomalous events etc to verify automatic logging captures everything Article 12(2) mandates. QA runs scripted scenarios each quarter... then reviews logs for completeness against checklist. Proving logs complete before regulator...\n\nWe need six subscales.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\nIs quarterly log fidelity stress test original? Probably unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Not expressed before? Might be uncommon but not ingenious. Stress testing logging is known practice. I'd say 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Or maybe 2 = interesting. The anchored: 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. I'd go 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance...\n\nQuarterly stress test is new process tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Score 2.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 easy low cost or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 significant change, or expensive or difficult\n1 totally infeasible\n\nQuarterly audit requires QA team script scenarios, review logs. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Probably 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 common strategies violate no norms\n3 somewhat uncommon but do not offend sensibilities\n2 offends somewhat\n1 radically violates\n\nInternal audit is acceptable. Common strategy. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Might be 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial)\n2 unreasonable or unlikely\n1 solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore.\n\nWill stress tests solve meeting logging mandate? It verifies logs capture everything, but does not actually implement logging. It assumes logging exists. The mandate is to technically allow automatic recording. Stress test doesn't create logging, just verifies. So effectiveness partial? It contributes to compliance assurance but doesn't ensure logging capability exists. Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (it helps, but only partial). Could be 2? Hmm.\n\nThe stated problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal is about verifying logs. It does help ensure compliance, but doesn't implement logs. It could be effectiveness 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 solves identified problem directly related\n3 solves implied problem related\n2 may have some benefit within special situation...\n\nThe proposal directly addresses ensuring logging meets Article12. That's directly related to mandate. So applicability 4? It does X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Stated problem is meet logging mandate. Verifying fidelity helps meet mandate. Might be 4.\n\nCheck definitions: applicability 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of the stated problem). Yes.\n\nThus ratings:\noriginality_evidence: ... score 3\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence... score 2\nimplementability_evidence... score 3\nacceptability_evidence... score 4\neffectiveness_evidence... score 3\napplicability_evidence... score 4\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each before number.\n\nProvide output with id and subscale_evidence then subscale.\n\nProbably format JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Might be plain text.\n\nWe'll output structured list.\n\nEnsure one sentence each.\n\nLet's craft evidence sentences.\n\noriginality_evidence: Quarterly injection of anomalies to test log fidelity is unusual for AI labs and shows imagination beyond routine compliance checks. -> score 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The test adds a new QA process but still treats compliance as a logging practice rather than changing how R&D and compliance relate. -> score 2\n\nimplementability_evidence: Running scripted scenarios quarterly requires QA time and checklists but uses existing systems, making it reasonably feasible. -> score 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: Internal audits for regulatory readiness are common and violate no legal, social or business norms. -> score 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Verifying log completeness helps ensure Article 12 obligations are met but does not itself create the required automatic logging capability. -> score 3\n\napplicability_evidence: The stress test directly targets the traceability and logging requirements of Article 12(2) for high-risk systems. -> score 4\n\nReturn.\n\n",
