. The proposal is research project - that is exploring whether idea might someday work, not committing to implementation. So effectiveness maybe lower? It will contribute but is only partial solution.\n\nLet's rate 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but only partial).\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem\nProposal directly addresses Article 12(2)(a) logging requirement for recording events relevant for identifying situations that may result in risk. That's directly related. So 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nResearch project to improve compliance is acceptable. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost non-radical changes\n3 = some changes reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change expensive difficult but not impossible\nProposal is research project at knowledge frontier, no published methodology, requires defining taxonomy, train detectors on synthetic and historical data, validate against post-market monitoring. That's significant change, expensive/difficult. So 2? Could be 2 = significant change or expensive or difficult but not totally impossible. Probably 2.\n\nMaybe 3 = some changes reasonably feasible. Research project is maybe not easy low cost. I'd say 2.\n\nLet's output.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating ... For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Might be plain text lines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P1286\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nOriginality evidence: The proposal claims no published methodology exists for operationalizing risk-relevant event detection and proposes novel frameworks beyond static thresholds.\nScore 4\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It weaves logging compliance into core R&D by developing risk-event detection as scientific research that feeds engineering specifications.\nScore 3\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Developing algorithmic detectors for Article 12(2)(a) events would improve traceability and post-market monitoring, but as a research project it provides only a partial, future capability rather than immediate logging compliance.\nScore 3\n\napplicability_evidence: The project targets the specific logging mandate in Article 12(2)(a) to record events relevant for identifying risks and supporting Article 72 monitoring.\nScore 4\n\nacceptability_evidence: Creating detection methods to meet EU AI Act logging requirements is legally compliant and socially unobjectionable.\nScore 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building a new taxonomy, training detectors on synthetic and historical failure data, and validating against post-market loops requires substantial R&D resources and time.\nScore 2\n\nReturn.\n\n",
