ning observability as emergent property of computation for biometric pipelines is unusual and shows imagination beyond typical compliance tooling.\n\nScore 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation\n1 = intervention not stated\n\nProblem: lab must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources.\n\nProposal designs framework for lifetime event logging emerges from system internal structure, makes timestamps etc side effects. That directly applies to logging mandate Art 12. Also addresses adaptation.\n\nSo applicability = 4? Solves identified problem directly related: do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Yes.\n\nEvidence: The framework directly targets automatic lifetime logging of timestamps, provenance and database references required by Article 12 for high-risk systems.\n\nScore 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill it solve? It proposes open-source framework and publish papers. Does it guarantee compliance for the lab's own system? Reference implementation over biometric verification pipelines. Might be effective but publishing papers is not immediate compliance. It may contribute.\n\nEffectiveness 3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Because building framework is research, not immediate deployment. Could be 4 if you think it would solve logging mandate if you could do it.\n\nThe proposal says design and release open-source framework... It converts constraint into contribution. Might solve problem without regard for workability: if built, lo