g implementation, not changing R&D. It uses compliance as differentiator. That's changing relationship. I think 3.\n\noriginality (1-4): 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination 2=interesting 1=common mundane boring\nPublic transparency whitepaper about logs is unusual? Many companies publish transparency reports. Might be unusual but not ingenious. Probably 3? Unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Could be 2 interesting. Let's pick 3.\n\nimplementability (1-4): 4 easy low cost non-radical changes 3 some changes reasonably feasible 2 significant change expensive difficult but not impossible 1 totally infeasible\nCreating quarterly whitepaper requires compliance officer + engineering + legal review. That's some work but reasonably feasible. Maybe 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy? Requires ongoing coordination. I'd say 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): 4 common strategies violate no norms 3 somewhat uncommon unusual strategies do not offend sensibilities 2 offends somewhat... 1 radically violates...\nPublishing logging details is acceptable? Might reveal sensitive info? Sample schemas ok. Probably acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Transparency whitepaper is common. Could be 4. Or 3 somewhat uncommon. I think 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: lab must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\nWill whitepaper solve logging mandate? It documents implementation but doesn't actually implement logging. It demonstrates traceability capability, reduces friction. But does it meet Article 12 requirement for automatic recording of events? No, it is communication about compliance, not actual logging infrastructure. So effectiveness is low. It may contribute to solution? It 