 new process elements. So 2 = slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). The stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal does logging-first to meet Article 12. That's direct. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? If you could do it, it would solve main problem. Logging-first ensures logs exist from first sprint, meets Article 12(2) triggers, avoids non-compliance window. Seems will solve. So 4.\n\nReturn rating with evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale in order listed: implementability, acceptability, originality, paradigm_relatedness, applicability, effectiveness\n\nProvide \"<subscale>_evidence\" then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably output JSON or lines. Echo id exactly.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P1808\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\n...\n\nLet's craft one sentence evidence each.\n\nImplementability evidence: The standard requires a new compliance gate and upfront log schema design before training, which is feasible with existing engineering processes but demands process change and documentation overhead. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Mandating logging upfront aligns with legal requirements and industry best practice for traceability, offending no norms or sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nOriginality evidence: Embedding logs at project kickoff as a signed design deliverable is unusual in R&D labs and shows imagination about shift-left compliance. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces new tools and process gates for logging but still treats compliance as logging practice rather than redefining R&D. Score 2.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly addresses the mandate to technically